{"id": "EUVD-2026-21944", "enisaUuid": "4e50b000-1d56-3f87-816e-466bf63a5d75", "description": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nbridge: mrp: reject zero test interval to avoid OOM panic\n\nbr_mrp_start_test() and br_mrp_start_in_test() accept the user-supplied\ninterval value from netlink without validation. When interval is 0,\nusecs_to_jiffies(0) yields 0, causing the delayed work\n(br_mrp_test_work_expired / br_mrp_in_test_work_expired) to reschedule\nitself with zero delay. This creates a tight loop on system_percpu_wq\nthat allocates and transmits MRP test frames at maximum rate, exhausting\nall system memory and causing a kernel panic via OOM deadlock.\n\nThe same zero-interval issue applies to br_mrp_start_in_test_parse()\nfor interconnect test frames.\n\nUse NLA_POLICY_MIN(NLA_U32, 1) in the nla_policy tables for both\nIFLA_BRIDGE_MRP_START_TEST_INTERVAL and\nIFLA_BRIDGE_MRP_START_IN_TEST_INTERVAL, so zero is rejected at the\nnetlink attribute parsing layer before the value ever reaches the\nworkqueue scheduling code. This is consistent with how other bridge\nsubsystems (br_fdb, br_mst) enforce range constraints on netlink\nattributes.", "datePublished": "2026-04-13T13:40:24", "dateUpdated": "2026-09-14T11:58:16", "baseScore": 0.0, "references": ["https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/610073ffb77ffd2b5eca182d2ac264de4834a175", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/ec8850be9b2b3beac1c7967d95f169dd2785979d", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/1ec86b4b9e28170a2565cf36f0e6e2b96b55134d", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/2120bd8546cd6a63c558d135773aa8f41c8259fc", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/630a15a31c2034b5b697f4aabc769b9d80d82446", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/e8ec80430bfa520e7352155d6ac632e527cba7aa", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/c9bc352f716d1bebfe43354bce539ec2d0223b30", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/fa6e24963342de4370e3a3c9af41e38277b74cf3"], "aliases": ["GHSA-644j-vc34-vf98", "CVE-2026-31420"], "assigner": "Linux", "epss": 0.09, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "1e17edb7-2a7e-38ba-98a5-715337964f4d", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "20f6a05ef63594feb0c6dfbd629da0448b43124d <e8ec80430bfa520e7352155d6ac632e527cba7aa"}, {"id": "33cbd7da-26d9-3586-9a83-4c2c1622fab7", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 0"}, {"id": "35b02867-b1e0-3ad2-b729-48fdf116eb82", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.12.92"}, {"id": "4052287d-1633-3717-ba2f-c72ab5c88c74", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.6.157"}, {"id": "591e1690-72d6-36f5-b99d-b5c75fc95740", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 5.10.270"}, {"id": "68b6c4f3-6a8f-3c70-bca2-7dafa27d4045", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 5.15.221"}, {"id": "7cacd5b2-361b-385a-ad7e-654a7eb78a1a", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "20f6a05ef63594feb0c6dfbd629da0448b43124d <2120bd8546cd6a63c558d135773aa8f41c8259fc"}, {"id": "91d7980c-9a45-3c6a-95c1-195d423002a0", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.18.34"}, {"id": "9f57b883-c855-3a50-b742-534338636649", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "20f6a05ef63594feb0c6dfbd629da0448b43124d <610073ffb77ffd2b5eca182d2ac264de4834a175"}, {"id": "a492837e-6c12-3fe9-91a1-a53abc342333", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "20f6a05ef63594feb0c6dfbd629da0448b43124d <c9bc352f716d1bebfe43354bce539ec2d0223b30"}, {"id": "a68fee7d-942a-34d9-8bca-44ef0a026c98", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "20f6a05ef63594feb0c6dfbd629da0448b43124d <1ec86b4b9e28170a2565cf36f0e6e2b96b55134d"}, {"id": "a7184768-93ae-3afb-b18f-f82ca1a2b8a3", "pr